  ($$forearm_mappings_v1$$, $$cat-135:forearm.br$$, $$cat-135$$, $$forearm.br$$, $$elbow.flexion$$, $$direct$$, $$dynamic$$, $$emg$$, $$individual$$, false, $$With the palms down the biceps is at a mechanical disadvantage and the brachioradialis's share of elbow flexion changes (M1 measured the difference in pronation), so the reverse curl is a direct brachioradialis exercise.$$, $$reviewed$$),
  ($$forearm_mappings_v1$$, $$cat-135:forearm.ecrb$$, $$cat-135$$, $$forearm.ecrb$$, $$wrist.extension$$, $$stabilizer$$, $$isometric$$, $$emg$$, $$individual$$, false, $$With the palms down the load pulls the hand toward the floor, and the wrist extensors hold the wrist straight for the whole set without moving it: isometric stabilization in the same bout as the dynamic elbow flexion (brief §11). Extensor activity with a pronated grip is documented in gripping (M2); it is not dynamic wrist-extension work.$$, $$reviewed$$),
